---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  Probador,
  GarmentIcon,
  ParenthesesCounter,
  DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS,
} from "../../components/cave/you-cannot-take-off-a-jacket-you-never-wore";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
import CheeseTick from "../../components/icons/CheeseTick.astro";
import CheeseCross from "../../components/icons/CheeseCross.astro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="You Can't Take Off a Jacket You Never Wore"
  description="Solving balanced parentheses with a dressing room metaphor"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      It might sound odd at first, but there is a close relationship between
      trying on clothes and knowing if a sequence of parentheses is valid.
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <aside class="notes">
        They look similar. They use the same symbols. But one of them breaks.
      </aside>
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> (()()) </code>
        <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> ())() </code>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">Intro here</aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <aside class="notes">
        We are given a line of parentheses. The question is simple: is this line
        well formed? Every opening parenthesis must be closed. And nothing
        should close before it's opened.
      </aside>
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> (()()) </code>
          <div class="fragment fade-in text-6xl" data-fragment-index="1">
            <CheeseTick class="w-24 mx-auto" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> ())() </code>
          <div class="fragment fade-in text-6xl" data-fragment-index="1">
            <CheeseCross class="w-24 mx-auto" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      The first thing we tried was counting. Parentheses come in pairs. So we
      count how many open, and how many close. If the numbers match... maybe we
      are done. And in many cases, we are. And when the numbers do not match,
      the sequence is clearly invalid. If the number of opening parentheses is
      greater than the number of closing parentheses, something is missing. If
      the number of closing parentheses is greater than the number of opening
      parentheses, something is extra. But then there is this.
      <!-- )( -->
      Same count. Nothing missing. Nothing extra. And still, completely broken. So
      counting helps. But it doesn't tell the whole story. Order matters.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="count"
        title="Count Opening & Closing"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      So instead of counting everything at the end, we follow the line as it
      unfolds. We keep track of how many opening parentheses we have seen that
      are still pending to be closed. If we try to close more parentheses than
      we have opened at any point, we know the sequence is invalid.
      <!-- ()) -->
      This works well for a single type of parenthesis. But what if we have more than
      one type? We have parentheses, brackets, and curly braces. It might seem that
      it works the same way.
      <!-- ([{}]) -->
      But look at this one.
      <!-- (] -->
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="balance"
        title="Tracking Pending to Close"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      So order matters, and type matters. We can try to keep track of how many
      opening parentheses of each type we have seen that are still pending to be
      closed. (] This example is no longer a problem. However, it's easy to find
      a new issue. ([)] We are trying to close a parenthesis when there is still
      an unclosed bracket. So we need to remember the most recent unclosed
      opener.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="multi-count"
        title="Counter Per Type"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Let's use an analogy to make this idea clearer. Think of a changing room.
      You walk in, and you put one t-shirt on. Then a sweater. Then a jacket.
      After looking at yourself in the mirror, you start taking your clothes
      off. You have to take off the jacket first. Then the sweater. Then the
      t-shirt.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold">The Dressing Room Analogy</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-12 mt-8 text-center">
        <div class="fragment flex flex-col items-center">
          <GarmentIcon
            client:load
            type="T"
            color={DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS.T}
            size={100}
            showLabel={true}
          />
          <p class="text-xl mt-4">T-Shirt</p>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment flex flex-col items-center">
          <GarmentIcon
            client:load
            type="S"
            color={DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS.S}
            size={100}
            showLabel={true}
          />
          <p class="text-xl mt-4">Sweater</p>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment flex flex-col items-center">
          <GarmentIcon
            client:load
            type="J"
            color={DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS.J}
            size={100}
            showLabel={true}
          />
          <p class="text-xl mt-4">Jacket</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 11: Valid nested sequence -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      It's not possible to take off the sweater before the jacket. The last
      thing you put on is the first thing you take off.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="stack"
        showTypeCounters={false}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 12: Stack insight -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This is exactly how our problem works. Instead of stacking clothes one on
      top of another, we are stacking opening parentheses. When we see a closing
      parenthesis, we need to make sure it matches the most recent opening
      parenthesis we have seen.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300">
        With multiple types, we need to remember
      </p>
      <p class="text-4xl font-bold text-amber-400">WHAT was put on last</p>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300 fragment mt-4">Not just count!</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 13: The tricky example intro -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold">The Tricky Example</h2>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300 fragment">
        The <strong>count per type</strong> might look balanced...
      </p>
      <p class="text-2xl text-red-400 fragment">
        ...but the sequence is <strong>impossible</strong>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 14: Tricky example demo -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="both"
        showTypeCounters={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 15: Tricky insight -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <div class="text-6xl">❌</div>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300">
        You can't remove the <strong>T-Shirt</strong> when the <strong
          >Sweater</strong
        > is on top!
      </p>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment mt-4">
        The counters say everything is fine (1 T, 1 S on)
      </p>
      <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-amber-400 fragment">ORDER MATTERS</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 16: The reveal intro -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold">The Final Reveal</h2>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300 fragment">
        Watch the labels transform...
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 17: Parentheses reveal -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="stack"
        showParentheses={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 18: Mapping explanation -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      Next, we define which closing parenthesis belongs to which opening one.
      Each closer knows exactly what it expects in the stack.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">The Mapping</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-12 gap-y-4 text-2xl">
        <div class="text-right">
          <code class="text-green-400">(</code> = PUT T-Shirt
        </div>
        <div><code class="text-green-400">)</code> = TAKE OFF T-Shirt</div>
        <div class="text-right fragment">
          <code class="text-blue-400">[</code> = PUT Sweater
        </div>
        <div class="fragment">
          <code class="text-blue-400">]</code> = TAKE OFF Sweater
        </div>
        <div class="text-right fragment">
          <code class="text-purple-400">{`{`}</code> = PUT Jacket
        </div>
        <div class="fragment">
          <code class="text-purple-400">{`}`}</code> = TAKE OFF Jacket
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 19: The insight -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <blockquote
        class="text-2xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl text-center italic border-l-4 border-amber-400 pl-6"
      >
        "Balancing parentheses is exactly like getting dressed in layers without
        breaking the laws of physics."
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      Let's see how this works in Python code. We start with an empty stack.
      This is where we keep the parentheses that are still open. In Python, we
      implement the stack as a list. Now we go through the input line, one
      character at a time. If the character is an opening parenthesis, we push
      it onto the stack. We are just remembering it. If the character is not an
      opening parenthesis, we assume it is a closing one. We assume no letters
      or numbers will appear in the input. Do we have something to close? If the
      stack is empty, we stop immediately. If there is something to close, we
      take the last thing we opened. We use pop to get and remove the last item
      from the stack. Now we check if the types match. If they do not, the
      sequence is invalid. We use the matching dictionary to see what we
      expected to find in the stack, based on the closing parenthesis we
      encountered. We repeat this until we reach the end of the line. At the
      very end, only one thing matters. Did we close everything we opened?
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    # STEP: We create empty stack to remember opening parentheses
    stack = []
    # STEP: Mapping of closing to opening parentheses
    matching = {
        ')': '(',
        ']': '[',
        '}': '{',
    }

    # STEP: Process each character in the input text
    for char in text:
        # STEP: If we see an opening parenthesis, we remember it
        if char in '([{':
            stack.append(char)
        # STEP: If we see a closing parenthesis
        else:  # we assume char in ')]}'
            # STEP: There must be something to close
            if not stack:
                return False

            # STEP: It must close the last opened parenthesis
            last_open = stack.pop()

            # STEP: And it must be the correct type
            if matching[char] != last_open:
                return False

    # STEP: At the end, nothing should be left open
    return len(stack) == 0`}
      `
    />
  </section>
  <section id="followup-code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      There is another way to solve this. Same idea. Different perspective. I'll
      leave it here... Leave a comment if you can figure out why it works.
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    stack = []

    for char in text:
        if char in "([{":
            stack.append(char)
        else:
            if not stack:
                return False

            last_open = stack.pop()

            diff = ord(char) - ord(last_open)
            if diff != 1 and diff != 2:
                return False

    return len(stack) == 0
`}
    />
  </section>
  <!-- Slide 20: Counter vs Stack -->
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      The time complexity is linear because we process each character in the
      input string exactly once. The space complexity is also linear in the
      worst case, as we may need to store all opening parentheses in the stack
      if they are never closed. So we have an efficient solution. The key
      takeaway is simple. With one type of parenthesis, counting works. But with
      multiple types, we need to remember the order in which they were opened. A
      stack is the perfect data structure for this job.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Counter vs Stack</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-50 text-xl mt-4">
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Single type</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Just count!</p>
          <code class="text-green-400 text-3xl block mt-4">( ( ) ( ) )</code>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Multiple types</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Need a stack!</p>
          <code class="text-3xl block mt-4">
            <span class="text-green-400">(</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">[</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`{`}</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`}`}</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">]</span>
            <span class="text-green-400">)</span>
          </code>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This was today's Cheese Byte. Remember, the code matters, but the idea
      comes first.
    </aside>
    <iframe
      src="https://cheesebytes.com/cave/parentheses-balancing/"
      width="1080"
      height="720"></iframe>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
